  nonce: 5a5d  # 5a5d
  data:  # 5a5d
    kind: invariant  # 5a5d
    title: "Transcripts are mode 664 while .env is 600 — the pipeline is a permission downgrade of the credentials it copies"  # 5a5d
    status: violated  # 5a5d
    priority: 2  # 5a5d
    parent:  # 5a5d
    tags:  # 5a5d
    - developer_experience  # 5a5d
    isbefore: []  # 5a5d
    duplicate_of: []  # 5a5d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5a5d
    pr_ref:  # 5a5d
    description: ""  # 5a5d
    fields:  # 5a5d
      statement: "A secret's copy is never more readable than its origin. VIOLATED: .env is mode 600 (owner only), but the transcript pipeline copies its values into files at mode 664 and a directory at 2775. Measured 2026-07-29: .env 600; .agent/.last_session_transcript.jsonl 664; .agent/.current_session_transcript.<sid>.jsonl 664; .agent/.archived-transcripts/ 2775 (setgid, group-writable). So any command whose output contains a credential -- git remote -v being the live case -- is silently republished from owner-only to group-and-world-readable. Confirmed live before remediation: those files held 138 occurrences of two live credentials. Scrubbing (WI-scrubber / scripts/scrub-transcript-corpus) removed today's occurrences but does NOT change the invariant: the next session writes 664 again."  # 5a5d
      root_cause: "Nothing in the pipeline sets a umask or an explicit mode for the files it creates. The per-session .current_* file inherits the process umask (002 in this environment) via the sync daemon, and .archived-transcripts/ was created setgid so archives inherit a group. scrub_secrets.py:scrub_in_place faithfully PRESERVES whatever mode it finds (and restores group ownership), so it propagates the downgrade rather than correcting it -- correcting it there would be the wrong layer anyway, since the file is already 664 by the time it is scrubbed. The fix belongs at creation: an explicit 0600 on the per-session transcript and on the archive writes."  # 5a5d
